I think a lot of companies are saying  well hey we're already doing that we  don't we don't make these decisions  based on someone's race or their gender  or their age or any other protected  characteristic but should we be doing  something else and sometimes people want  to throw training at it and there's  unfortunately not a lot of data that  says training is very effective and even  the federal government has said that hey  I'm Dave Rubin and it's Small Business  Week here on the Rubin report joining me  today is Wendy Motor le co-owner of HR  Northwest from Seattle Washington Wendy  welcome to the show thank you very much  Dave so I know that legally I have to  ask you about what life is like in  Seattle right now with your autonomous  zone before we get into anything else of  course of course we are outside of the  autonomous zone but it seems to be  changing by the day yes are you hearing  from people that have businesses that  are in the autonomous zone or around the  autonomous zone about how they can treat  their employees or ask people to come to  work or are people not wanting to come  to work or anything else like that yes  we have clients that are outside of but  near the autonomous zone who are dealing  with the need to protect their employees  from from some some from some of the  rioting that is happening so they are  dealing with opening up late leaving  letting people leave early and otherwise  allowing employees to get out so they  can board up windows and that kind of  thing  yeah so I thought you'd be interesting  to talk to for a couple reasons because  your business sort of deals in many ways  with the business of business how do you  handle these strange situations not just  you know autonomous zones and protests  and that sort of stuff but obviously  kovat asking employees to come back to  work when everyone hears these mixed  messages from the government and from  their employers and everything else so I  guess first can you talk a little bit  about your company's sort of general  ethos and then how you apply that to  these strange times that we're in right  now sure yes what we do is we are HR  professionals we help our clients make  good business decisions with respect to  employment related matters so we try to  help our clients strike that balance  between taking care of employees and  doing right by them and then also doing  right by the business so that this  business can continue to be sustainable  and exist for a long period of time we  help our clients navigate very tricky  laws especially the more recent laws  like the FF CRA the familiesfirst Crone  a vibration response act that just  recently passed yeah you know back in  late March and there are there's just a  tangle of complexities around that so  how do you guys learn on the fly because  I know that looking at these PPE  documents and everything we signed up  for and from talking to other small  businesses it's like whether you're an  HR professional or a small business  owner or a lawyer I mean this is  seriously complex stuff that often  changes I mean every week it seems like  the rules and regulations change it is  it's changing constantly we we subscribe  to a number of different alerts with the  government with with law firms and then  we have some HR related resources that  we buy into as well but then a lot of it  is our team just keeping on top of it  reading up on the regulations themselves  and and then we're constantly kind of  discussing it with each other to make  sure that we're we're all staying on the  same page about how these should be  interpreted and utilized so what are the  types of thing  that businesses are going through right  now like some of the consistent  questions that you guys are getting as  businesses are opening up yeah a huge  question that keeps coming up is that my  employee is scared to come to work they  are they are immune compromised maybe or  they just don't want to come to work  right now because they're afraid of  coronavirus or they have some other  reason that they just feel really  uncomfortable coming in so that's been  the biggest challenge and I would say  most of the time we're seeing employers  giving people a leave of absence at  least for a few months while we all sort  of weather this storm of course it's  understandable but at some point  businesses get to a point where they say  hey this is causing serious staffing  issues now and I have to bring my people  back are you hearing a ton this is a  slight offshoot of the of the corona  thing specifically but are you hearing a  ton from employers or employees that are  now being confronted with all these  diversity and inclusion memos and all of  these things sort of a set of guidelines  that it's always seems sort of unclear  where it comes from or how they have to  apply it where suddenly you know just  hiring someone on on the basis of their  qualification is no longer good enough  yeah that is a really difficult question  we've had clients come to us and ask hey  what should we be doing around diversity  equity inclusion and should we be doing  more so we're definitely having that  conversation I personally really like to  look at the data and the research around  it and not just assume it's a hiring  problem or it's a promotion problem but  really try to try to dig into the  details I think that certainly makes  sense for companies to look at whether  they have any specific biases in their  hiring processes if it's if that's clear  that you are making these decisions  based on characteristics other than  their qualifications for the job then of  course a company should absolutely look  at that but I think a lot of companies  are saying well hey we're already doing  that we don't we don't make these  decisions based on someone's race or  their gender or their  age or any other protected  characteristic but should we be doing  something else and sometimes people want  to throw training at it and there's  unfortunately not a lot of data that  says training is very effective and even  the federal government has said that so  it is an area where we're still  grappling we're talking to various  diversity experts out there to try to  figure out what it what is the right way  to approach this problem have you seen  the general sort of business of HR  change say over the last decade or so  where now it seems like so much of it is  public where maybe back in the day if  people had a problem you complain to  your superior they take it to HR there's  some sort of process maybe it's good  maybe it isn't versus these days where  it's sort of like if something happens  to someone at work what's the first  thing you do you screenshot the email  yeah you text it to somebody at BuzzFeed  next thing you know the boss has been  fired before the company's done any sort  of research or you know an investigation  of their own yes that has added a huge  new component so now instead of  employees may be off of work having a  few beers at a local bar might have a  conversation together about something  work-related now that conversation ends  up getting broadcast and it is causing I  think a lot of harm in a lot of ways  because people aren't aren't filtering  what they're saying the information is  getting back to their boss that they  called their boss a jerk on social media  and it's causing a lot of issues and  sometimes employees get fired and that  firing is upheld when they're all  they're saying is my boss is a jerk and  sometimes the language is a lot more  colorful than that yeah that must be  interesting for you that you deal with  all sorts of different companies that  have different cultures as opposed to  being just an in-house HR team that  you're dealing you're dealing with one  boss or one culture you're dealing with  all sorts of different ones right we are  yes all sorts of different businesses  all sorts of different cultures and  environments and so we r